==English==

===Etymology===
{{unc|en}}. First used in the 18th century. Perhaps a [[metathesis]] of {{suffix|en|film|s}} {{suffix|en||y}}; or related to {{m|en|flimflam}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈflɪmzi/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-flimsy.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# Likely to [[bend]] or [[break]] under [[pressure]].
#: {{syn|en|weak|shaky|flexible|fragile}}
#: {{ant|en|robust|strong|sturdy}}
#: {{ux|en|He expected the '''flimsy''' structure to collapse at any moment.}}
#* '''1715''', {{w|Richard Brinsley Sheridan}}, ''{{w|The Rivals}}''
#*: All the '''flimsy''' furniture of a country miss's brain.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[weak|Weak]]; [[ill-founded]].
#: {{syn|en|weak|feeble|unconvincing|unfounded|unsubstantiated}}
#: {{ant|en|well-founded|substantiated}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''flimsy''' excuse}}

